  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 357 AM CDT Fri Sep 11 2026\n\nCurrent surface analysis places low over northern Manitoba and \nSaskatchewan with secondary low over western South Dakota. Cold\nfront continues to drop through northwest into north central\nNorth Dakota. Upper level analysis places broad ridge over the\nMidwest, with trough over the northwestern CONUS resulting in\nwest/southwest flow over our area, though a few short waves\nswinging around low along the northern Manitoba/Saskatchewan\nborder continue to be noted in the flow.\n\nFor today, cold front will continue to drop through the area\nwith winds becoming northerly and breezy in its wake. In\naddition, notably cooler air will move in behind the front. \nHigh temperatures over our area will range from the mid 60s \nover northwestern North Dakota to the 80s over the southern \nJames River Valley. A broad short wave swinging around the upper\nlow to the north pushes into our area, which may result in some\nmodest afternoon showers. Chances settle towards the south \ntowards evening. \n\nOn Saturday, surface high pushes towards the area, with notably\ncooler temperatures (highs about 10-15 degrees below average). A\nshort wave moving through overnight may result in some rain\nshowers over southern parts of the area. In the meantime, a\ncompact surface low pushes onshore over the Pacific Northwest. \n\nOn Sunday, modest ridging develops to the west ahead of the\naforementioned low, quickly passing over the area as the upper low\nslides over the Rockies and a lee slide surface low develops. \nSystem then pushes east-northeast over the Northern Plains and \nCanadian Prairies, bringing widespread high rain chances \n(~70-100 percent) to our area Sunday night into Monday. Latest \nNBM guidance shows a 50 percent or great chance of seeing a half\ninch of precipitation along and north/east of the Missouri \nRiver, with a 70 percent or greater over north central North \nDakota. \n\nSystem then exits the area late Monday with drier westerly flow\nfor Tuesday. Thereafter, guidance is having trouble with some\nkey features, but overall the pattern suggest occasional modest\nprecipitation chances. As for temperatures, NBM continues to\nexpress low confidence given the notable spread in possible\nhighs/lows.",
